hw/ - FPGA hardware for the UART-attached CGRA
==============================================

Synthesisable VHDL-2008 for a 4x4 mesh of 16-bit processing elements, a
UART, and the protocol controller that stitches them together. Simulate with
GHDL; build/flash a bitstream with Vivado in batch mode (no GUI).

Default board is nexys_a7 = Digilent Nexys 4 DDR / Nexys A7-100T (xc7a100t);
basys3 (xc7a35t) is also supported via BOARD=basys3.

Layout
------
    rtl/    synthesisable sources (2 packages + UART, PE, array, controller, top)
    sim/    GHDL testbenches
    con/    board constraint files (.xdc) -- PHYSICAL only (pins/IO/config)
    scr/    Vivado batch scripts; timing + floorplan factored into sourced procs
    perf/   Fmax trajectory tracking (PERFLOG) -- where the timing wall is

Build (make -f hw.mk <target>; BOARD defaults to nexys_a7)
---------------------------------------------------------
    sim              analyse + elaborate + run every testbench (GHDL)
    lint             analyse the RTL alone (syntax/elaboration check)
    wave             run tb_cgra_top, dump build/tb_cgra_top.ghw (gtkwave)
    synth            synthesis-only gate: fail on error/critical warning (fast)
    fmax             binary-search the max closing frequency + report the wall
    bit  [PERIOD=]   synthesise a bitstream, timing-gated at PERIOD ns (def 10)
    sta  [PERIOD=]   static timing analysis gate: fail unless timing is met
    prog             flash via the Vivado hw_server
    prog-ofl         flash via openFPGALoader (no Vivado)
    clean
  Knobs: BOARD, PERIOD=<ns>, FLOORPLAN=1 (apply scr/floorplan.tcl in bit/sta/fmax).

Timing, a priori
----------------
scr/constraints.tcl constrains the clock (period is the knob) and false-paths
the async UART/button/LED pins. `make bit` refuses to emit a bitstream unless
setup and hold timing are met; `make sta` is the same check on its own; `make
synth` is the quick synthesis-only pre-check. So a bitstream that exists is one
that will run on the board -- certified by STA, not by staring at a waveform.

The one non-obvious constraint: the PE ALU (operand mux -> DSP multiply ->
accumulate -> op mux) is too deep to settle in 10 ns on a -1 Artix-7, so the
controller issues 'step' on alternate clocks (cgra_ctrl.vhd S_RUN) and
constraints.tcl declares the datapath a two-cycle multicycle path. This closes
timing without pipelining the DSP, so one step still yields one result and the
fabric stays bit-identical to lib/src/emu.c. Drop either half and setup fails by
~1.8 ns at 100 MHz.

Pushing Fmax
------------
`make fmax` binary-searches the highest clock the design closes at and names the
limiting path, so each optimisation starts from the actual wall (see perf/PERFLOG
for the trajectory). FLOORPLAN=1 boxes the PE array over a DSP column to shorten
the datapath routes. Because the fabric only advances when the UART-bound
controller pulses 'step', a higher clock is headroom + a known limit, not raw
throughput -- but it is the discipline that keeps the datapath honest.

Design in one breath
--------------------
uart_rx/tx move bytes; cgra_ctrl is the protocol FSM (v2, checksum-protected)
that loads per-PE config words, injects edge operands, pulses 'step' and reads
the register file back; cgra_array wires G_ROWS*G_COLS instances of pe into a
nearest-neighbour mesh. Everything runs in one clock domain.

Protocol note
-------------
The command bytes and config-word layout in rtl/cgra_pkg.vhd are the contract.
They are mirrored by the host in lib/include/cgra.h and lib/src/emu.c. Change
one, change all three, or the emulator and the silicon drift apart.
